      "id": 1,
      "text": "Springtown was founded in 1763 when Abraham Funk bought 300 acres of land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springtown%2C_Bucks_County%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first recorded white settler in the area was Englishman George Wilson, who arrived in 1728 and built a log hut by Cook’s Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.springfieldbucks.org/our-community/about/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Springtown’s historic inn, the Springtown Inn, still serves travelers at the junction of Routes 212 and 412.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springtown%2C_Bucks_County%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Springfield Township Bucks County Historical Society, founded in 1957, preserves artifacts and documents in a historic building in downtown Springtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/26967"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every summer, the Historical Society hosts a Civil War Commemoration that draws history enthusiasts from across the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/26967"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Springtown sits at the confluence of Cook’s Creek and its tributary Silver Creek, draining eastward into the Delaware River.",
